---
import Layout from '../../../../layouts/Layout.astro';import '../../../../styles/equipment.css';
const p='/equipment/14-04/',brochure='https://www.ntu.edu.sg/media/docs/librariesprovider121/research-/sgsr/facilities/lab2/rheometer-mcrxx2-brochure-r1.pdf',tri='https://www.prolabbg.com/uploads/e6c975d21fab91d68830e1f2f76255931af41ed6.pdf',study='https://doi.org/10.3390/nano10040683';
---
<Layout pageTitle="MCR 302 tribology bench" subtitle="" wide={true} description="Measure signed sliding force on a simulated three-contact fixture."><script is:inline>window.mcr302Module=import('/equipment/14-04/model.mjs');</script><article class="equipment" id="mcr"><nav class="eq-nav"><a href="/simulations/equipment/14-04/">← Equipment 14-04</a><a href="/simulations/equipment/">Register</a><a href="#mcr-refs">References</a><a href="#mcr-proof">Verification</a></nav><header class="eq-mast"><p class="eq-meta">14-04 / MECHANICAL CHARACTERIZATION</p><h2>Friction in two directions</h2><p class="eq-lede">The Anton Paar MCR 302 drives a ball across three fixed contacts. Run one oscillation, then select the half-cycle: each contact still carries 2 N, but its friction force changes sign.</p></header>
<div class="grid"><section><p class="eq-meta">01 / INSTRUMENT</p><h3>MCR 302 · T-PTD 200</h3><div id="tr-view" aria-label="Orbitable 3D tribometer"></div><small id="tr-viewstate" role="status">Loading metric model…</small><div class="tools"><button id="tr-far">Whole machine</button><button id="tr-near">Three-contact fixture</button><button id="tr-engage">Raise ball</button><button id="tr-load">Remove triad</button></div><p>The ball, triad and instrument display are model targets. The contact view is magnified by camera placement, not rescaling. Raise the spindle before exchanging the inert phantom.</p></section>
<section><p class="eq-meta">02 / SETUP</p><h3>Drive a sliding cycle</h3><label>Specimen ID<input id="tr-sampleId" value="INERT-TRIAD-01" maxlength="60"/></label><div class="fields">{[['axialN','Axial load (N)',4.242640687119286,3,20],['mu','Prescribed kinetic μ',.2,0,1],['angleDeg','Angular amplitude (°)',30,1,90],['frequencyHz','Frequency (Hz)',1,.1,3]].map(([k,t,v,min,max])=><label>{t}<input id={'tr-'+k} value={v} min={min} max={max} type="number" step="any"/></label>)}</div><div class="tools"><button id="tr-run">Run cycle</button><button id="tr-reverse">Start in reverse</button><button id="tr-reset">Reset example</button></div><p id="tr-notice" role="status">Inert constant-friction phantom loaded.</p><p>Fixture temperature is held at an assumed 23 °C. Changing μ prescribes a different synthetic surface pair; this bench does not infer material μ from first principles.</p></section></div>
<section><p class="eq-meta">03 / READOUT</p><h3>Resistance on the rotating ball</h3><canvas id="tr-plot" width="620" height="194" aria-label="Signed friction per contact versus time"></canvas><p id="tr-read" aria-live="polite"></p><label>Inspect acquired sample<input id="tr-index" type="range" min="0" max="200" value="0" disabled/></label><div class="tools"><button id="tr-half">Inspect half-cycle</button><button id="tr-save" disabled>Export trace JSON</button></div></section>
<section id="mcr-refs"><p class="eq-meta">04 / REFERENCE &amp; SCOPE</p><h3>What the geometry represents</h3><p><a href={tri+'#page=10'}>Manufacturer tribology photograph and specification table, p. 10</a>: the original 302 (not 302e) stand, drive, sample cup and face controls. The <a href={brochure+'#page=19'}>MCR xx2 series brochure, specification sheet</a> gives nominal 678 × 444 × 586 mm (interpreted H × W × D), 200 mN·m maximum torque and up to 50 N axial force. The <a href="https://www.anton-paar.com/ca-en/products/details/mcr-tribometers/">accessory description</a> distinguishes the T-PTD 200 ball-on-three-plates from the T-PID/44 pin-on-disk. The GLB is an original simplified reconstruction, not manufacturer CAD. Coarse 18 mm retract, fixture housing, controls, ports and pad lengths are inferred.</p><p>A <a href={study}>published MCR 302/T-PTD 200 experiment, §2.4</a> documents a 12.7 mm ball and the axial-to-contact force decomposition; <a href="https://mediatum.ub.tum.de/doc/1624677/1624677.pdf">TUM, Fig. 2.6</a> documents a 12.7 mm ball and 5.5 mm pads. The present fixed, dry, constant-μ pair and reported results are synthetic, not reproduced experimental data.</p></section>
<section id="mcr-proof"><p class="eq-meta">05 / MODEL &amp; VERIFICATION</p><h3>Three force components; one torque</h3><p>Three equal contacts lie 120° apart, normal tilted β=45° to vertical. Local normal N=Fz/(3cosβ), track radius r=Rsinβ. For θ(t)=±A sin(2πft), local tangential velocity is v=rθ′. The signed force on the ball is −sign(v) μN per contact. Their local force vectors do not add along one line, but their torques do: τ=3rF. At the instant of rest the kinetic term is reported as zero and recovered μ is null, not an estimate of static friction.</p><div class="over"><table><thead><tr><th>Independent default check</th><th>Expected</th><th>Computed</th><th>Tolerance / result</th></tr></thead><tbody>{[['Axial 3√2 N → normal/contact','2 N','2 N'],['Forward → reverse friction','−0.4 → +0.4 N','−0.4 → +0.4 N'],['|τ| = μFzR (45°)','5.388154 mN·m','5.388154 mN·m'],['Recovered μ = |F|/N','0.2','0.2']].map(([k,e,a])=><tr><th>{k}</th><td>{e}</td><td>{a}</td><td>Pass · 10⁻¹² SI before rounding</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div><p>Limits: fixed radius 6.35 mm, three equally loaded contacts, 3–20 N axial, μ=0–1, 0.1–3 Hz, 1–90° drive, 201 samples per cycle. The normal force and coefficient are prescribed inputs; the positive/negative force and torque are model outputs, not calibrated readings. No stick-slip, wear, thermal response, lubrication, viscoelastic rheometry, inertia or instrument feedback. Incompatible loads/settings or an empty/raised fixture prevent the run.</p><p><b>Handoff:</b> <a href={p+'example.json'}>Example trace</a> uses <code>bts.tribology-trace.v1</code>. It carries specimen and parent/acquisition revision, three contact IDs, fixture dimensions, SI units, trace time in seconds, signed angular motion, sliding speed, normal load, friction (N) and resisting torque (N·m). Coordinate and sign convention are embedded. No compatible downstream station is assumed.</p><div class="tools"><a href={p+'build-model.py'} download>Editable model</a><a href={p+'mcr302.glb'} download>Download GLB</a><a href={p+'model.mjs'}>Solver / actions</a><a href={p+'xr.json'}>Metric checks and VR targets</a></div><small>Named metre-scale actions and poses are reusable in a VR workshop; no physical Quest test or native VR session is claimed.</small></section></article></Layout>
